resource based policy VS Identity based policy for cross accont access:-
 They both will provide a way to acceess a aws resource from one account fby the aws role/user from other account but there mechanism has a very significant diffrence:-
 Suppose you wants to access the aws resource name "muf_resource" from aws account "mufazzal" by the service "vend_service" in account "vendor" 
 Identity based policy:-
  Here you will create a role in "mufazzal" like "access_for_vend" which will be assumable by a role/IAM user in account "vendor".
  suppose "access_for_vend" [of mufazzal account] have below permission:-
   {
      Allow, "s3:*", "dynamo:*",
   } 
  So this role of mufazzal account "access_for_vend" can perform any operation on S3, dynamo that lies in mufazzal aws account.

  And, iam user/role "i_am_vendor" [of vendor account] have below permissions: 
   {
      Allow, "ec2:*", "lambda:*".  "sts:assumeRole"
   }
  So this role/user of vendor "i_am_vendor" can perform any operation on Ec2, lambda that lies in vendor aws account.

  but what will happen if "i_am_vendor" assume the role "access_for_vend"? 
   1. will it have collective permission to do operation on Ec2, lambda of vendor account as well as s3, dynamo of mufazzal account
   2. will it have collective permission to do operation on Ec2, lambda of vendor account only
   3. will it have collective permission to do operation on s3, dynamo of mufazzal account
   ANS:-
    once the role is assumed, It can perform operation on s3 and dynamo of mufazzal account only. it will loose all orignal permission granted 
    to "i_am_vendor" via its permission policy and can have only permission that granted via assumed role's permission policy.
    So right ans is 3

   This is the big limitation of IAM based cross account access. once access given by assumed role, the principle will losse all the orignal permission 
   granted by its orignal policy. this cause limitation of what that role could have done with assuming it.
